# The Seventh Majlis

Sayyid Abbas's account

When Sayyid Abbas had read out this testament, he began to describe how he himself had been brought up. He said: until I was eight or nine my mother did not put me in a *maktab* (a neighbourhood school); she taught me herself: three hours before midday and two hours after; those five hours were my hours of study. Apart from that, from morning until ten at night all my time was spent on other things. After the morning prayer I would go out with a servant to take the air in the open country, and would come straight back as soon as the sun rose. As soon as I came in I bathed in hot water, put on clean clothes, and ate whatever breakfast was ready. By then it was usually seven o'clock. Now came my time for study. Until ten Amma-jan would teach me without a break. After that I and my nurse's elder son Ghulam Imam, who was my foster-brother and a year older than me, would play in the stable yard until eleven; two or four boys of the neighbourhood would come to play with us too. At eleven I came in from there and had my meal, then read the newspaper until twelve. After that, there was a calligrapher who lived next door to us; I would go to his house and practise *nastaliq*. At one I came back from there. After the midday prayer Amma-jan taught me again until three. As soon as it struck three I was let off study. Now until five I busied myself with my gun. Islam Beg, who had been a servant in our house since Abba-jan's time, was a very good shot; he would take me to the stable yard and teach me to aim. Meanwhile I would also say the afternoon prayer. After five, if it was the hot season, I would bathe; if not, I went as I was, riding out of the city on horseback to take the air. In the evening I came back from there and said the sunset prayer, then had my dinner. After dinner I strolled in the courtyard for a while, and by then it was time for the night prayer; first I did my ablutions and said the prayer, then read a book for an hour. After that I went and sat with Amma-jan. Now, until ten, I would listen to her talk. Sometimes she would ask me something by way of a test, sometimes tell a story, sometimes give me some advice. As soon as it struck ten I went to my own bed and slept.

Now hear the details of it. Amma-jan always had thick cloth made up for me, and she used to say that, in the first place, fine cloth needs a great deal of care; secondly, in it the body does not stay under control; besides, one does not sweat properly in it; and it is a rule that as long as the pores stay open, a person is spared most illnesses. But she was most insistent that I wear clean clothes, and she would mock in every way the people who go about dirty and unkempt although they have clothes, so that I should come to loathe dirty clothes. And she always dressed me plainly; I cannot remember ever having worn even a cap with braid on it, or having had trimming at the collar or edging on the hem. If ever some boy came with someone, dressed in clothes with gold trimmings, she would say to them in front of me: "My dear, dressing your boy in clothes like these, are you going to make the poor thing [TN: literally "his enemies", a polite euphemism] into a eunuch or a woman?" And whatever the season, summer, winter or the rains, she always made me wear a vest under my *angarkha* (a long coat), and was insistent that my chest should never be exposed anywhere. She was also extremely strict about bathing; except when I was ill or indisposed, I do not remember a single day when I missed my bath. She used to say that the dirt that forms from sweat every day blocks the pores; the cure for it is for a person to bathe daily, so that dirt never settles on the body.

As for eating, my orders were: eat little bread. Eating a lot of grain certainly makes the body fat, but it weakens the heart and brain. Milk and meat, though, are no harm; eat as much of these as you like. And the order was: don't eat your fill at one sitting; eat four times a day if you like, but eat while still a little hungry. Eating your fill, in the first place, makes food slow to digest; secondly, it makes you sluggish, your hands and feet will not obey you, your mind grows dull, and all sorts of illnesses arise. And chew each mouthful well before you swallow it. And she would keep me from drinking too much water as one keeps someone from a bad deed, and she used to say that a person who does not restrain his thirst is no different from an animal; people often feel a false thirst, and drinking water then is very bad; after a meal one should hold off for at least an hour; and one should not drink water immediately on waking, after walking, or after exertion.

As for play, it was like this. A little way from the house we lived in there was a very large mud-walled enclosure; the cow, the buffalo and the horse were tethered in it, and their fodder and chaff were kept there too. There we had made a wrestling pit; there we played and there we wrestled. The servant who stayed with us had Amma-jan's orders that we should play no game except bat and ball, *kabaddi*, running, or wrestling. Islam Beg, who taught me to shoot, knew a great many fancy tricks with a gun; but Amma-jan had told him: just teach him to aim straight; I don't want any tricks and the like. In the same way the riding-master who taught me to ride was also a great master of his art, and knew a thousand tricks of horsemanship; but he too had orders: get the boy to the point where his thighs grip the horse firmly; where he understands the use of the reins; where his seat is not irregular; where he is not at a loss at any pace, trot, gallop, walk or turn; where he does not lose his seat when the horse is going at full speed; where he comes to know the temper and ways of horses; where in bridling, saddling, girthing, putting on the head- and heel-ropes and currying he does not depend on the groom; and where he learns how to treat the illnesses horses commonly suffer from. In the same way my teacher, from whom I took correction in handwriting, could also write many hands: *naskh*, *gulshan*, *ghubar* and *tughra*, and besides these he was a master of many other scripts; but Amma-jan's instruction was: teach him to write *nastaliq* as well as you can; it is enough that no one turns up his nose at his writing and that it can be read easily; I don't want to make a great calligrapher of him.

The room Amma-jan had given me to live in was built to one side of the courtyard of the house. The other rooms in our house were all built in the old style, but my late father had had this room built for himself in a new fashion. The air came into it freely from all four sides, east, west, north and south; round it ran a verandah a yard and a half wide, and on all four sides of it a strip of ground three yards wide was left for a garden. I had orders to sit and live in this room day and night, and my books, my bed and my bedding were kept in this room too. There were other very fine rooms in the house, but she seldom let me sit anywhere else, and used to say that the air in all the rooms stays shut in all the time, and air has the property of water: just as water that stands still goes bad in a few days, so in a room whose air is not let out every day illness arises; and the air will only be let out if there is a way for fresh air to come in from the other side; that is why physicians have laid down that a room should have a way for air to come and go on both sides. My son, the people of former times were simple folk; they knew only the style of building that had come down from of old; but your Abba, who learned the new sciences, had become very skilled in these matters. Several times he meant to pull this house down and build a new one in his own fashion, but it was a memorial of our elders, and I held him back from that by force; then, having no choice, he had this room built.

Until I went to the *maktab*, Amma-jan's chief concern was to set my character right. The truth is that there is no harder task than forming the morals and habits of one's children; but she had found such a knack for it that, had I been worth anything, I should have profited greatly from her upbringing. In the first place, her own habits and character were such that the mere sight of them taught one humanity. Secondly, by changing and changing again she had picked out servants, inside and outside the house, such that, men and women alike, they were all the very picture of loyalty; and once they had understood that the lady of the house cared for nothing so much as for the boy's upbringing, then Amma-jan herself kept no such watch over me as they did. Whether I was at home or went out, from all sides I heard the same voice: "Look, young master! What are you doing? That is not nice; that is very bad; this is the thing to do; that is not the thing to do." Thirdly, Amma-jan treated me in such a way that I had to do as she said in every way. I do not remember Amma-jan ever giving me so much as a slap; indeed, apart from once or twice, perhaps she never even scolded me. In spite of this I stood so much in awe of her that I could not say a word against her. The whole thing was that, from the time I came to my senses, she never let me laugh out loud in front of her, let alone play; I always sat before her in the manner that was proper for sitting before her; and kissing and hugging and petting me all the time was not her way either. Besides this, the maidservants were always frightening me with her displeasure; and since I had never once tasted a beating, the very word made my soul shrink; for this reason I did whatever she told me. Fourthly, it was her habit that if I did something wrong she would pretend not to know at all, as if she had not noticed; but if she saw the same fault two or four times, she would take me aside and quietly explain to me: "My son, look: this time I have hidden your fault; if you do it again I shall shame you in front of everyone; if you have any regard for your honour, then never go near this thing again."

Until I was seven or eight I was always being told: eat and wear whatever food and clothes are given you at home; those who get a craving for fine food and fine clothes, when hard times come, either beg, or steal, or gamble. Do not reach for a second mouthful until you have swallowed the first. When you eat, do not let your clothes get covered in lentils and curry. One should not reach for the food before one's elders. Things that disgust others, such as shaking your hands, chewing your food noisily, dropping food from your mouth on the cloth, belching loudly, or taking a mouthful and putting it back in the dish, should not be done at the dining cloth. In the same way she kept me from spitting towards anyone, blowing my nose in front of anyone, sitting with my back to anyone, taking whatever someone offers without a moment's ceremony, holding out my hand to anyone, butting into anyone's conversation, chattering nonsense, swearing, lying, taking oaths, sleeping at midday, not getting up early in the morning, stooping in the dark without feeling one's way, sitting in the sun, warming myself at the fire, walking without looking ahead, walking very fast or very slowly, not planting my feet firmly, and going about bare-bodied.

Above all she was always correcting my mistakes of speech. First of all she taught me the rules of how one should speak to one's elders and how one should talk to those younger. I was always told never to address even the lowliest sweeper as *tu* (thou); when you speak, say *tum* (you). Elders, and equals one has newly come to know, should be addressed as *ap* (Your Honour) or *hazrat* (Sir); and with someone of the same age or rank, with whom one is on easy terms, you may say *ap* or *tum* as you please. And one should call an elder's coming *tashrif lana* (to grace with one's presence), and his going *tashrif le jana*, and his sending for one *yad karna* (to remember); his health *mizaj-e-mubarak* or *mizaj-e-ali* (your blessed or exalted temper); his greeting *taslim*, *adab*, *kornish* or *bandagi*; his house *daulat-khana* (abode of fortune); his speaking *farmana* or *irshad karna* (to command); his children *sahibzada* or *sahibzadi*; his name *ism-e-mubarak*, *ism-e-sami* or *ism-e-sharif* (blessed, lofty or noble name); and his home town *watan-e-maluf* (familiar homeland). In the same way one should call one's own coming *hazir hona* (to present oneself), one's speaking *arz karna* (to submit), one's house *gharib-khana* (poor abode), and one's children *banda-zada* or *banda-zadi* (servant's son or daughter); and to call oneself *ham* (we), or to add Sayyid, Shaikh or Mirza to one's own name, is a very foolish thing.

Besides this, whenever a wrong word slipped from my tongue she would correct it for me. I remember that one day I said *phul-walon ki sail* [TN: for *sair*, "the Flower-sellers' Outing", the annual festival at Mehrauli near Delhi]. When Amma-jan heard it she smiled and said: "My son, all the other good things of this city went long ago; all that was left, for what it is worth, was its battered language, and now you want to lose that too. Listen: from today remember that whenever you hear a new word from someone, ask us whether the word is correct or not. Some mistakes are such that only ignorant people of the bazaar make them and nobody else, such as *sail* for *sair* (outing), *mijaz* for *mizaj* (temper), *nukhsa* for *nuskha* (prescription), *qabutar* for *kabutar* (pigeon), *phattar* for *patthar* (stone), *darwza* for *darwaza* (door), *bzar* for *bazar* (market), *na-mahrum* for *mahrum* (deprived), *be-nahaq* for *nahaq* (wrongly); and besides these there are hundreds of other words; to use them is a great fault. And some mistakes are on the tongues of everyone, educated and uneducated alike, such as *tabe'dar*, *badshahat*, *khairao*, *chaku*, *baqrid*, *murghan*, *shabash*, *shabashi*, *lachar* and so on; there is no harm in mistakes like these. [TN: Hali lists everyday forms that are, strictly, irregular by the rules of Persian and Arabic; he does not give the "correct" forms.] Besides these, there are many peculiar words that only women use and men do not, such as *nauj* (God forbid), *dur-par*, *chhain-phuin*, *ab se dur*, *chal dur*, *sidharna* (to depart), *mu'a* (wretch), *pichhal-pai* (a she-ghoul), *wari* (I'd give my life), *achchhi*, *ujra* (wretched), *marne-joga* (fit to die), *bhaiya*, *bhaina*, *nighora*, *jana*, *penda* [1], *bua*, *mardua*, *rasna-basna*, *nigora*, *jhulsa*, *bhar men jae* (to the furnace with it), *chulhe men jae* (into the stove with it), *ag lago* (may fire take it), *dar-gor* (to the grave), *bakhtawari*, *bodli* and so on. [TN: Most of these are women's oaths, curses and terms of endearment; several have no exact English equivalent.] Mind you never come out with words like these in front of anyone; people will not say anything to you, but they will say that the boy has been living with his mother and has started to talk like a woman. In short, a person should first set his own language right and then think of learning other languages; otherwise it will be as the proverb says: 'The crow tried to walk like the swan and forgot its own walk.' The rule for it is simply this: when you hear a new word from someone, first see whether that person's speech carries authority or not; if he is an uneducated man or a man of the bazaar, never use the word until you have heard it from the lips of some capable person. And listen: language does not come right merely by study; it comes right by paying attention. Educated people who do not take care always use wrong words; and those who do pay attention, even if they are uneducated or come from outside, learn the language of the city in a few days."

Besides this, she was always teaching me useful things at all hours of the day: the meanings of the Arabic or Persian words used in Urdu; the names of the Arabic, English, Persian and Hindi months; the details of the Hijri, Christian, Fasli and Bikrami eras; how to find a date from an almanac; the forms of address for people high and low; how to wind clocks and watches and read the hands; the rules for reading the thermometer, the compass, the telescope and the microscope; the knack of reading a map; and scores of other things like these she would tell me by word of mouth.

Besides this, an old lady from Baghdad had happened to come to the city; she spoke both Arabic and Persian very well, and what she wanted was that, if someone would take on the cost of her food and clothes, she would settle here; for in the first place she was very old, secondly her home was very far away, and thirdly she had not a cowrie left for the journey; she thought she might as well spend the days of life left to her here. She was staying in the city at the house of Nawab Zulfiqar-ud-Daula. When Amma-jan heard her praised, she invited her one day on the pretext of a meal; and found that both her languages were indeed excellent. Amma-jan took on her expenses and kept her in our house, and treated her so kindly that she had no wish to go anywhere else. At odd hours, whenever I was free of other tasks, I would sit and talk with her. Although I did not study Arabic and Persian very much, in her company I came to speak both these languages so well that, just as my tongue never falters in my own speech, it is the same in these two languages. Well, so much for my training in speech; now hear about my studies.

When I entered my seventh year and had learned one *sipara* (one thirtieth part of the Qur'an) and the *Panj Sura* (five chapters of the Qur'an) by heart, Amma-jan started me on Urdu and arithmetic. To describe in detail how she taught me until the age of nine would take a long time; but the few things by which my zeal for study grew day by day were these. In the first place, from the day I began to read books, Amma-jan kept nothing in my room except the equipment for study. Throughout the room there were either books, or maps of countries hung on the walls, or a writing board, inkpot, pens, paper, slate, sponge, pencil and rubber, or the writing desk, or my bed and bedding, a lamp-stand, the floor-covering and its white sheet, or a clock; apart from these there was nothing; it was as if the whole room was teaching me all the time. Besides this, when Amma-jan began to teach me, for ten or fifteen days my heart was not in my studies as it should have been; as long as she sat teaching me I went on reading out of fear of her; as soon as she got up and went off on some errand, I began to play. To rouse my spirit of rivalry she made Ghulam Imam, my nurse's elder son, my fellow pupil. He was older than me, and his mind and memory were very good too. Now I began to worry that I might be put to shame [2] by him in something; and once or twice, when Amma-jan praised him for his reading and said nothing to me, my heart received another blow; now my eagerness began to grow by the moment. Besides this, there was such a knack to the testing that we had to work hard whether we liked it or not. It was Amma-jan's rule to ask more than she taught. In the first place, whenever she sat down to give a lesson, she would always first ask five or seven questions on the previous lesson; secondly, at the evening hour when I sat with her she would often question me too; then Thursday was set aside especially for examinations. But she asked questions so easy that we had no great difficulty answering them, for on days when we did badly in an examination our minds grew even duller from vexation. And she often asked questions that needed a longer answer from us; this also improved our speaking. And to improve our speaking she would ask us the meaning of the book again and again; until we could express the meaning clearly, she would not go on; and this also showed whether we had understood or not.

We learned to read Urdu prose simply by reading the newspaper. A copy of a newspaper used to come to Amma-jan from Agra; she would read it first and then give it to me; whatever part I read each day, I had to tell her what it meant at night. Partly from fear of this and partly from eagerness for the latest news, we read the newspaper with great care and attention. The paper came every seventh day; on that day I was in such a state of excitement that I kept going out and coming in again, waiting for the postman.

My honoured mother was very particular that I should read the text of a book correctly and properly. First she would have me spell out every letter; when I had worked out the whole lesson she would read each phrase separately herself and show me; if we read badly, or ran one phrase into the next, or left out an *izafat* (the Persian linking vowel), she would show us again once or twice in her own voice; and if even then we did not read well, she would mimic us to shame us. I had a strong habit of reading in a low voice; she would go and sit far away from us and say: "My son, read so that I can hear too." Willy-nilly I had to read aloud. And if I began to read too loudly she would say: "My son, a book should be read the way one talks to someone; think of the book as a friend sitting in front of us, to whom we are talking."

Things that only had to be learned by heart, with nothing to understand in them, she made us learn so thoroughly that we became worn out [3] with repeating them. I remember well that Amma-jan took such pains in making me learn my multiplication tables that I became convinced I should be made to learn these same tables all my life. Every day we learned four or five tables and recited them, but she would always say: "No, young man, they are not on the tip of your tongue yet; learn them some more." I used to say to Ghulam Imam: brother, the day I am rid of these tables, I shall hand out an anna's worth of sugar drops.

When we did not answer a question thoughtfully but blurted out any nonsense, she would find some trick or other to make us think harder. Sometimes she would say: "Whoever gives the right answer will get the afternoon off tomorrow." Sometimes she would say: "Whoever pleases me now will get something very nice from me." And when we thought hard and gave the right answer, she kept the promise she had made and said: "Well, sir! If I hadn't tempted you, would you ever have thought about it?"

I remember one day we had just come in from outside after watching a conjuror's show; as soon as we came in, Amma-jan had us write out some numbers. My mind was on the show; who was going to write? Well, I wrote something after a fashion, but apart from one or two numbers they were all wrong. She had me write them three or four times, and still they were not right. She said: "Come, my son! Write all these numbers quickly, and I'll tell you something very funny." We at once thought hard and wrote them all correctly. Then I said: "Amma-jan, what is it? Tell me now." She said: "My son, have you been to see the cook-woman, and how she is?" I said: "Is everything all right? What happened?" She said: "You were out at the time; a peacock was dancing on the roof here. The cook-woman was carrying the pot of ghee from the kitchen to the storeroom and kept turning round to look at the peacock as she went; there was a five-seer weight lying somewhere ahead; she tripped over it and fell flat on her back; the pot was gone, the ghee was wasted, and on top of that she hurt her hands and feet. Just go and see her." I said: "The cook-woman is a real fool." Amma-jan said: "Young man, it is not only her; whenever a person keeps his mind on something else while doing one thing, this is what happens to him. I have told you again and again that when you are asked something, keep your mind on that; at such times put games and shows out of your thoughts."

In short, the story is very long, but the gist of it is that by the age of nine Amma-jan had me know one *sipara* and the *Panj Sura* by heart on the tip of my tongue; I learned to read and understand Urdu prose as well as one could wish; and she taught me in Persian the *Gulistan*, the *Bustan*, the *Mala Budda* and the *Akhlaq-e-Muhsini*; in Arabic two or three primers each of grammar and syntax; in arithmetic proportion and vulgar fractions; and besides these the geography of India and the history of India. In that time I also made fair copies of all the writing boards in *nastaliq*, and of some calligraphic pieces too.

But since Amma-jan had still not put me in a *maktab*, there was talk throughout our whole family: "Look, the late Mir Amjad Ali's boy has still not been put in a *maktab* by his mother; if Mir Sahib were alive today the boy would have got far; you see, he has fallen into the snare of women; only God can make anything of this boy through education." Next to our house were two other houses, joined to it; in one lived Dadi-jan and in the other my aunt. The three houses had separate doors, but inside there were connecting windows, through which the women came and went all the time. My grandmother came over only now and then, but my aunt paid a visit every day; and her daughter Khadija Khatun, whom my honoured mother herself had educated, used to spend the whole day at our house whenever she came from her in-laws. I remember well that I was a little under nine and it was the month of Ramazan, when one day my aunt was sitting at our house and Apa Khadija had come that day too. In the course of conversation she said to Amma-jan: "My dear, how old will Sayyid be?" She said: "He will enter his tenth year on the eighteenth of Baqrid." My aunt said: "Bi, I know: perhaps you have no intention of educating him? It is the custom of the world that as soon as a child's *Bismillah* ceremony is done, he is put in a *maktab*. Your child, God keep him, is now entering his tenth year, and to this day you have not shown him a teacher's face. Tell me honestly, what do you mean to do? Are you going to keep the poor boy ignorant, God forbid? In our family no one has ever been ignorant till now." Amma-jan said: "But, sister, he is studying; how else do people study? Now, God willing, when he enters his tenth year I will put him in a *maktab* too." My aunt said sourly: "My dear, your ways are different from the whole world's. If children could study at home, why would anyone send them to a teacher? We have never heard of anyone's child studying like this. Just tell me too: at what time, God keep him, does he study? Whenever I hear, I hear only that the boy has gone out for the air, or he is bathing, or he is playing *kabaddi* or bat and ball in the stable yard, or wrestling, or running about with the children, or shooting at targets, or he has gone out riding; or I hear that he is sitting at home listening to stories from his Amma-jan, or sitting cudgelling his brains with the old lady. You never scold him, you never threaten him; are these the ways [4] to study? Sister, a child can never learn to read and write until he has been kept under a teacher's restraint for twelve hours together. In my opinion you should send him to school from tomorrow. He has run wild long enough; it is not good to give so much rope. Don't you know how much my brother, God rest him, loved learning? His soul will be grieved at the boy running wild." When she heard this Amma-jan smiled and said: "*Baji-jan* [5], if you and I and my Sayyid live ten more years and it pleases God, I will show you the fruit [6] of this running wild. If your brother were alive today he would praise my efforts. You are my elder, my senior; your word is on my head and eyes; but forgive the liberty: you have no experience of this. Educating children is a very difficult task; in it both haste and delay are bad; it is not good to scold a child too much, nor to spoil him too much; one should neither work him so hard that he becomes distraught, nor give him so much freedom that he runs wild. Where study is concerned a child is a wild animal; to tame him and bring him under control is no child's play. Why look far afield? It was only yesterday that Khadija Khatun studied with me; from the age of five she was educated in my own way; today, by God's grace, show me any other girl in our whole community with as much learning, humanity and skill in everything as she has. Ask her yourself: on what day did I ever beat her? On what day did I threaten her? Apart from three or four hours, I never made her work hard at her studies, and until she was eight I put no restraint on her of any kind; what game is there that she did not play?" My aunt said: "Bi, what has my daughter to do with it? She was only a girl: whether she studied or not, it made no difference. In our own family there are hundreds of uneducated girls; will they never find husbands [7], God forbid? Will no one ever let them sit in company? But a gentleman's son must never, God forbid, remain ignorant. And, my dear, take it well or ill, I have never in my life seen a boy taught by his mother. Well, each to her own judgement; that is how it seemed to you; you are hardly going to listen to anyone now." Amma-jan said: "Tell me honestly, *Baji-jan*: when Khadija Khatun was five or six months old, how did you give her her *ghutti* (a medicinal infusion given to infants)?" She said: "Why, the way it is given, how else?" Amma-jan said: "When she began to cry while drinking it, did you still go on giving her the *ghutti* regardless?" She said: "Bi, as people do, I would give her to the nurse to feed for a while; when she was quiet, I would begin giving it again." Amma-jan said: "And now, when she is ever unwell, God forbid, do you still give her the cooling drink with a spoon, or have her drink a whole cupful at once?" My aunt said: "My dear, are you all right? Am I mad, to have it given to her with a spoon now?" Amma-jan said: "Then understand just this. In my view, teaching a child and giving him bitter medicine are the same thing; that is why, until he understands the benefits of learning, he should be taught in the same way as *ghutti* is given, a little at a time; and when his heart begins to tire of study, he should be diverted with games and play. And I have left him no time for play anyway. These very things that you count as running wild are, if you ask me, even more useful than study. As it is, he studies five hours a day without a break; he learns to speak Persian and Arabic from the old lady; and the hour or half-hour he sits with me at night is pure gain too: in the first place, I keep asking him these same points of his studies by way of examination; besides that, from whatever story I tell, he also gains some lesson or other. It is human nature that you may tell a person a thing a hundred thousand times, or forbid him something, and it does not even touch him; but when he hears the good or bad of something in a story, it is engraved on his heart.

I remember that one day Amma-jan told this tale in front of me. A gentleman's son was setting out for his wedding; he borrowed a shawl from a friend of his for an hour or two. When the wedding party arrived and halted at the bride's house, a man asked: 'Excuse me, sir, which one is the bridegroom?' The man from whom the groom had borrowed the shawl happened to be standing there too; he said: 'Sir, the bridegroom is the one sitting there, but the shawl is borrowed.' The poor bridegroom was mortified [8] to hear this, took off the shawl and handed it back to him. The man said: 'Brother, I was at fault this time; forgive me this once, but it won't happen again'; and gave the shawl back to the bridegroom. After a little while another person came along asking the same thing; he said: 'Sir, this is the bridegroom sitting here, and the shawl is his own too.' The bridegroom took off the shawl and threw it down, and said: 'God save us from favours from such a petty man.' He again begged his pardon and gave him the shawl. After a little while a third came asking; he ran up quickly and said: 'Sir, this is the bridegroom, and there is no question of the shawl.' At last, having no choice, the bridegroom handed him back his shawl, and never asked him for anything again as long as he lived. From the day I heard this tale I have never felt like reminding anyone of a favour I have done. It so happened that once, while Amma-jan was still alive, at ten or eleven at night, the Mughlani's daughter was thirsty and asked for water; I was sewing something at the time; I got up and gave her water. Amma-jan happened to be awake at the time too; in the morning she asked me, on purpose: 'Well, miss! You sat sewing [9] at night, and could not get up to give the nurse's daughter some water?' I bore her displeasure, but the words would not come out of my mouth that I had got up and given her water at that very moment; I said to myself, I must not appear petty. When Amma-jan saw that she would not say it for this reason, she said: 'My child, when some blame falls upon oneself, there is nothing wrong in mentioning a kindness one has done'; and saying this she hugged me. [TN: In this passage Sayyid Abbas's mother is still speaking; "Amma-jan" here is her own mother, and the "I" is Zubaida Khatun.]

And as for what you said, that a woman has no need of education: if you ask me, women need education even more than men. *Baji-jan*, I make you swear by my own head: when a letter comes from Bhai-jan and there is no one in the house to read it, do you grow anxious or not? And if no one could be found for hours to read it, and something urgent were written in it, what trouble you would be in! If a man is uneducated, at least he can go somewhere and have it read."
